[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "ayatori-coscientist-iteration-07", :doc/title "Co-Scientist iteration 07 - the novelty chain is four links deep at the fold threshold, so the grouping change is worth one round trip and the sealer should not be built", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-07.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 07 - the chain is four links deep, and that shrinks the prize to one round trip\n\n> Iterations 05 and 06 argued about a chain 64 links deep. It is 4. Everything\n> below follows from measuring the depth instead of quoting an ADR for it.\n\n## L. The depth, measured in the repository that owns the chain\n\nkotoba-lang/kotobase-peer `test/kotobase_peer/novelty_chain_depth_test.cljc`\n(landed 581f142d) walks the real novelty chain:\n\n    unfolded tx    1   4   16   17   32   64   128\n    chain depth    1   1    1    2    2    4     8\n    segment sizes [1] [4] [16] [1 16] [16 16] [16x4] [16x8]\n\n**Depth is ceil(tx / 16), so at the fold threshold of 64 the chain is FOUR links.**\n\nTwo accepted ADRs disagree about this by 16x. root ADR-2608021000 says the novelty\ncons chain has depth equal to the number of unfolded transactions; root\nADR-2608160100 measured ceil(unfolded/16). The second is right, and the first\ndescribes the shape this codebase LEFT: `novelty-segment-size` is 16, and its own\ndocstring says \"1 was the original shape: one block per unfolded transaction\".\nThe batching landed as the fix FOR the measurement ADR-2608021000 recorded.\n\n**Iteration 05 quoted the defect as if it were the code**, in its own words: \"One\nlink per tx, and a tx is a commit. So the novelty chain crosses commits by\nconstruction ... and 0.50x is the figure for it.\" The crossing is real - the four\nsurviving segments at tx=64 were written by commits 63, 47, 31 and 15, four\ndifferent ones, so W = 1 does put exactly one link per pack. The DEPTH was wrong,\nand iteration 06 inherited it.\n\n## K. Absolute round trips at that depth\n\nRatios are the wrong unit here. Per window the packed arm pays a FIXED 3 (2 open +\n1 catalog); that amortises over 64 links and does not over 4.\n\n    unfolded  links | per-object  commit-pack  window-pack | win vs p-o  vs commit-pack\n          16      1           2            4            4      -2 trips        0 trips\n          17      2           4            8            5      -1 trips        3 trips\n          32      2           4            8            5      -1 trips        3 trips\n          64      4           8           16            7      +1 trip         9 trips\n         128      8          16           32           11      +5 trips       21 trips\n        1024     64         128          256           67     +61 trips      189 trips\n\n**At the fold threshold, window packing saves ONE round trip. Below three links it\nLOSES up to two.** The crossover is at 3 links, which is iteration 02's N/P > 3\nrule stated in the unit that matters, because links ARE N/P under window grouping.\n\nIteration 06's headline - the chain walk goes from 0.50x to 1.91x - is a true\nstatement about a 64-link chain and it is not a statement about production. At\nproduction depth the same change is worth one round trip, and the 1.91x is only\nreachable when fold has fallen 1024 transactions behind.\n\n## What this changes about the design\n\n**Do not build the window sealer.** Iteration 06 named it as the next step and\niteration 05 predicted N/P = 64 for it. The prediction was right about N/P and the\ndepth makes it worth ~1 round trip on a cold read at threshold, against a\nmechanism that has to buffer across isolates and can only seal when a window\nCLOSES - and iteration 06 section H measured that unsealed tail costing back\n0.12-0.19x of the win. A deferred, cross-request sealer is not the right price for\none round trip.\n\n**ADR-2608160100's fold-first conclusion stands, and this is the second\nmeasurement to support it.** That ADR declined to write compaction on the grounds\nthat fold removes the numerator rather than dividing it. The same argument\napplies here: fold is what holds the chain at 4, and at 4 the grouping question is\nworth single digits. The lever is the fold threshold, not the pack boundary.\n\n**The write side is still the whole win, and it is unconditional.** Iteration 05\nmeasured 144 puts -> 16 puts (9x) with byte overhead SHRINKING as packs grow\n(+2.2% to +0.7%). That is per commit, it needs no window, and it is available with\nthe grouping the code already has.\n\n**So the cutover trade is now a pair of measured numbers rather than a ratio.**\nTurning `KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES` on buys 9x fewer PUTs per commit and costs +8 round\ntrips on a cold chain read at the fold threshold (16 vs 8). Both numbers are small\nand neither is a ratio to argue about; whether the trade is worth taking is a\ndeployment decision about write volume versus cold-read frequency, and it can now\nbe made from two integers.\n\n## What was corrected, and where\n\n  - iteration 05 section E: the novelty chain does NOT have one link per tx.\n  - iteration 06: the 1.91x is a 64-link figure; at production depth it is +1 trip.\n  - root AGENTS.md and ADR-2608160100 decision 4: the default flipped to the\n    novelty window on iteration 06's numbers, and now carries the depth caveat.\n    The grouping to declare is still the window where a window exists, because\n    nothing about that got worse - the PRIZE got smaller.\n\n## Seeds for iteration 08\n\n  - The fold threshold is the actual lever and nothing has swept it. Depth is\n    ceil(tx/threshold-ish/16); the round trips a hydrate pays are a function of\n    it, and ADR-2608160100 measured novelty at 94-98% of the sequential term at\n    512-2048 unfolded. Sweep the threshold against fold cost (O(graph_shard) per\n    fold) and find where the product is minimised.\n  - Everything is still one host and an in-memory range server. An open costs 2\n    reads here; over a real HTTP Range server it may not cost 2 round trips.\n  - The backlog regime (1024 unfolded, +61 trips) is the one case where the\n    grouping is worth real money. Measure how often production is actually in it\n    before dismissing it - the answer is in the fold telemetry, not in a bench."}]
